{"text": " Prices are falling in some places. Just look at Austin. It's about 27% below its 2022 peak. But it's not because of extreme price cuts across the city. Austin added 30% to its housing supply in about a decade. The rest of the country added about 9%. So what happened is a flood of brand new homes hit the market, supply demand shifted, and the prices fell. Now look at Hartford, Connecticut. Nobody's idea of a boom town. There's no massive building wave happening in Connecticut. But Zillow calls it the hottest housing market in the country this year, and Hartford's prices are up more than 20% above the 2022 peak. But that's because the supply of homes is constrained. It's the same country with the same interest rates, but opposite results. Where supply exploded, prices fell. Where it didn't, prices kept climbing. And the supply that moves prices doesn't come from the people who already own homes. It comes from the one seller who can't afford to wait, the builder.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.7, "text": " Prices are falling in some places. Just look at Austin.", "tokens": [50363, 29431, 389, 7463, 287, 617, 4113, 13, 2329, 804, 379, 9533, 13, 50511], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13215942649574547, "compression_ratio": 1.6808510638297873, "no_speech_prob": 0.0032197872642427683, "words": [{"word": " Prices", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.32, "probability": 0.951485276222229}, {"word": " are", "start": 0.32, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.9977808594703674}, {"word": " falling", "start": 0.58, "end": 0.86, "probability": 0.9978585839271545}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.86, "end": 1.04, "probability": 0.992102324962616}, {"word": " some", "start": 1.04, "end": 1.2, "probability": 0.9987577199935913}, {"word": " places.", "start": 1.2, "end": 1.54, "probability": 0.9956609606742859}, {"word": " Just", "start": 2.02, "end": 2.08, "probability": 0.9517636895179749}, {"word": " look", "start": 2.08, "end": 2.26, "probability": 0.99721539